>
  <h2>What people mean by “raw feeding”</h2>
  <p>
    Raw feeding usually describes diets built from uncooked animal ingredients: muscle meat, edible bone,
    organ meat such as liver and kidney, and sometimes eggs, fish, or small amounts of vegetables and
    oils. Advocates argue that dogs evolved eating whole prey and that lightly processed food better
    matches canine anatomy. Critics, including many veterinary groups, point to documented nutrient
    deficiencies in ad hoc raw recipes and to bacteria that survive on raw meat. The debate is not
    about whether dogs can chew raw food—they clearly can—but whether a given household can consistently
    deliver a safe, complete diet.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two labels dominate online forums. Both approaches require math: calcium and phosphorus must stay in
    the right ratio, especially for puppies, and vitamins such as D, E, and B complex do not appear in
    adequate amounts in muscle meat alone. WSAVA’s Global Nutrition Guidelines stress that homemade diets,
    raw or cooked, should be formulated by someone trained in veterinary nutrition, not copied from a blog
    post. AVMA and FDA highlight pathogen risk in raw animal-source protein.
  </p>

  <h2>BARF vs prey-model ratios (common frameworks)</h2>
  <p>
    These splits are community frameworks, not AAFCO or WSAVA feeding standards. Use them only as a
    starting vocabulary, then have a veterinary nutritionist balance the recipe.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Framework</th>
        <th scope="col">Typical split (as fed)</th>
        <th scope="col">Notes</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Prey model</td>
        <td>~80% muscle / 10% bone / 10% organ (often ~5% liver)</td>
        <td>Little or no plant matter; micronutrients still need planning</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>BARF-style</td>
        <td>Often ~70% muscle, ~10% bone, ~7–10% organ, plus plants/oils</td>
        <td>Plant share and supplements vary widely by recipe author</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Portion size is a separate question from balance. Many raw feeders use a percent-of-body-weight
    convention (often ~2–3% for adult maintenance); that is not a veterinary standard—see the{' '}
    <a href="/tools/homemade-dog-food-calculator/">homemade dog food calculator</a> and{' '}
    <a href="/guides/homemade-dog-food-calories/">homemade calories guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How raw compares to commercial food</h2>
  <p>
    Complete and balanced dry or wet foods sold in the United States carry an AAFCO nutritional
    adequacy statement, meaning the recipe meets published minimums (and some maximums) for dogs at a
    stated life stage, either through laboratory analysis and formulation or through feeding trials.
    That does not guarantee every brand is ideal for every dog, but it does mean someone calculated
    iodine, zinc, copper, and dozens of other nutrients you cannot see in the bowl. Raw feeding shifts
    that responsibility to you or your nutritionist.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some owners feed raw for perceived benefits: smaller stools, shinier coat, or relief from mild
    food sensitivities. Research is mixed, and perceived benefits often track with higher protein and
    fewer fillers rather than rawness itself. Conversely, studies and surveillance reports have found
    Salmonella, Listeria, and E. coli on commercial raw products and in home kitchens that prepare raw
    meals. The FDA’s consumer guidance on raw pet food describes those risks for both pets and
    immunocompromised people in the home. Freezing does not sterilize meat; it only slows bacteria.
  </p>

  <h2>Food safety rules that actually matter</h2>
  <p>
    If you handle raw meat for your dog, treat the kitchen like a clinic. Use separate cutting boards
    and utensils, wash hands before touching human food, and disinfect counters after prep. Store thawing
    meat on the lowest refrigerator shelf so drips do not contaminate other items. Discard leftovers
    that sit at room temperature for more than an hour. Children, elderly adults, and anyone with a
    weakened immune system should avoid handling raw pet food altogether when possible.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Thaw in the refrigerator, not on the counter.</li>
    <li>Feed in a washable bowl; pick it up after the meal instead of leaving raw residue out.</li>
    <li>Do not rinse raw meat in the sink; splashing spreads bacteria.</li>
    <li>Wash your dog’s face and paws if they dive into a messy raw meal.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Tell your veterinarian that you feed raw. If your dog develops vomiting or diarrhea, mention diet
    history; clinics may use special handling for fecal samples from raw-fed dogs. Routine fecal testing
    for parasites is sensible because raw wild game and some meats can harbor worms.
  </p>

  <h2>Building a balanced plate</h2>
  <p>
    A common beginner mistake is feeding mostly lean muscle meat. That diet is high in protein but low
    in calcium unless edible bone or a calibrated supplement provides it. Organ meats supply vitamins A
    and B, but liver in excess can deliver too much vitamin A over time. Fish or fish oil may be needed
    for omega-3 fatty acids depending on the rest of the recipe. The National Research Council’s Nutrient
    Requirements of Dogs and Cats lists the targets formulators use; hitting them by eye is difficult.
  </p>
  <p>
    Start with calories, not just percentages. An inactive 20 kg dog might need roughly 800 to 1,000 kcal
    per day; a working dog needs more. The <a href="/tools/dog-food-calculator/">dog food calculator</a>{' '}
    estimates daily energy from weight and activity so you do not overfeed dense raw blends. Then map
    those calories onto your recipe’s ingredients. The{' '}
    <a href="/tools/homemade-dog-food-calculator/">homemade dog food calculator</a> helps translate
    ingredient weights into portion sizes once you have a vet-approved formula— it is a portion tool, not
    a substitute for formulation. For a deeper walkthrough of RER/MER kitchen maths, see{' '}
    <a href="/guides/homemade-dog-food-calories/">homemade dog food calories</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Bones, teeth, and choking</h2>
  <p>
    Raw feeders often use raw meaty bones for dental chewing and calcium. Size must match the dog: a
    bone should be large enough that the dog gnaws rather than swallowing it whole. Never feed cooked
    poultry or pork bones; heat makes them splinter. Even appropriate raw bones can crack teeth,
    especially in aggressive chewers. If your dog guards food, resolve that behavior before introducing
    high-value raw items. When in doubt, ask your vet whether ground bone in the mix is safer than whole
    pieces for your individual dog.
  </p>

  <h2>When raw is a poor fit</h2>
  <p>
    Households with toddlers crawling on floors where raw juice dripped, or with family members on
    chemotherapy, should think carefully about pathogen exposure. Dogs with pancreatitis, certain kidney
    conditions, or on immunosuppressive drugs may need cooked or prescription diets instead. Large-breed
    puppies are particularly vulnerable to calcium excess or deficiency; growth should be managed with
    precise formulas, often a commercial large-breed puppy food, unless a board-certified nutritionist
    designs otherwise. Pregnant and nursing females have elevated needs that are easy to miss on an
    improvised raw menu.
  </p>

  <h2>Practical path if you still want to try</h2>
  <p>
    First, book a consultation with a veterinary nutritionist (many offer telemedicine) and bring your
    dog’s weight, body condition score, and lab work if available. Second, choose either a commercial
    raw product that bears a complete-and-balanced statement for your dog’s life stage, or a custom
    recipe you follow exactly—no swapping proteins weekly without recalculating. Third, run a
    three-month check-in: weigh the dog, review stool quality, and adjust calories using the calculators
    linked above. Fourth, stay open to hybrid feeding; some owners feed raw once daily and kibble once,
    which still demands balance across the day but may reduce prep load.
  </p>

  <h2>Bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Raw feeding basics are really food-safety and nutrition basics. Dogs can thrive on well-designed raw
    diets and on well-designed kibble; they can suffer on poorly planned versions of either. Read FDA
    and WSAVA guidance with an open mind, insist on completeness the way AAFCO statements do for
    commercial food, and use our tools for energy math while a professional handles the micronutrients.
    That combination is slower than buying a bag of food, but it is the difference between a hobby and
    a diet your dog can live on for years.
  </p>
